# Trusted user provisioning

The REST print includes PlatformAdmin-only tenant administration and Cognito user
invitation endpoints. User provisioning is disabled until operator setup is complete.
The Angular monolith print provides the matching screen at /admin/tenants.

## One-time operator setup

1. Apply the generated PostgreSQL migrations. The first tenant is
   {{ model.default_tenant_id }}. For related services, use the same tenant directory
   identifiers; independently creating tenants allocates different IDs. Operate one
   authoritative onboarding API per user pool. Replication into other service
   directories must be completed before those services can serve the new tenant.
2. Configure the Cognito pool with the string custom attribute custom:tenant_id.
   Remove its write permission from EVERY untrusted app client. Do not map a
   federated identity provider attribute into this authoritative attribute.
3. Deploy the separate Identity Token Enrichment print and attach its V2_0
   pre-token-generation trigger. Use an Essentials or Plus pool. Confirm real
   access tokens contain tenant_id. The Lambda is not deployed by this REST print.
4. Configure the pool for administrator-created users with email invitations and
   temporary passwords. The welcome template must include the username and
   temporary-password placeholders. Configure sender/SES delivery and test it.
   V1 onboarding uses lowercased email as username (maximum 128 characters);
   pools using phone-only or incompatible usernames require adaptation.
5. Give ONLY the API workload role these Cognito actions on the specific user pool:
   cognito-idp:AdminCreateUser, cognito-idp:AdminGetUser, cognito-idp:ListUsers.
   The generated runtime does not need AdminUpdateUserAttributes, group membership
   administration, or pool administration. No AWS keys or client secret go to Angular.
6. Set Forge:Provisioning:Enabled=true. UserPoolId and Region are generated from
   the existing Cognito configuration and must match Forge:Oidc:Authority.
   Use environment overrides when deploying; do not put credentials in configuration.
7. Set the Angular app's apiUrl to this API and allow its exact origin in
   Forge:Cors:AllowedOrigins. The existing auth interceptor supplies the bearer token.

## Bootstrap the first platform administrator

Use a trusted operator AWS console/CLI session, outside the application:

- Create the initial administrator with custom:tenant_id set to the seeded tenant
  GUID at creation time, before attempting sign-in.
- Create the PlatformAdmin Cognito group if needed and add that administrator.
- Complete the invitation/password flow and sign in to the generated Angular app.
- Open /admin/tenants. The backend enforces PlatformAdmin for every operation.
  Ordinary Admin membership does not grant platform administration.

There is no public bootstrap endpoint and invitations cannot grant roles.
If you change Forge:Authorization:PlatformAdminGroup, update the Angular route
permission and dashboard visibility check to match. UI guards are for navigation;
backend authorization remains authoritative.

## API and retries

- POST api/v1/tenants with a name creates the tenant in PostgreSQL.
- POST api/v1/tenants/{id}/users with an email creates a Cognito user, assigning the
  route tenant GUID in the SAME creation request and requesting an email invitation.
  It requires an active tenant. It does not mark email verified or migrate aliases.
- A successful new creation returns 201. Retrying the same normalized email returns
  200 with alreadyExists=true only when its existing tenant AND email match.
  It sends no new invitation. Unassigned users and users belonging to another tenant
  require operator review; they are never adopted or reassigned.
- POST api/v1/tenants/{id}/users/resend-invitation with username explicitly resends
  for an enabled user in that tenant awaiting their first password. It resets the
  temporary password. Delivery is not guaranteed by an API success response.
- GET api/v1/tenants/{id}/users?nextToken=... returns users and nextToken.
  Cognito cannot search custom attributes, so the server filters a single bounded
  page. Empty pages can still have a nextToken; continue until it is absent.
  Large pools should add a durable membership index in a later version.

There is no distributed database/Cognito transaction: tenant creation is separate
from user creation. A failed invite leaves the tenant intact. A timeout or delivery
failure may occur after Cognito creates the account. Retry the SAME email first,
then explicitly resend if appropriate. Never delete a tenant or account as automatic
compensation. User assignment and the account are created together; there is no
intermediate account that this API later assigns to a tenant.

Audit events record actor subject, tenant, user subject when known, and operation.
Route them to the deployment's durable logging retention policy. No passwords,
tokens, email request bodies, or raw AWS exception messages are logged here.
Tenant lifecycle updates can race with an invite; runtime directory admission still
denies inactive tenants. Suspending a tenant blocks API access after directory-cache
expiry; it does not itself disable Cognito sign-in or revoke tokens.

## Verification before production

Run the generated ProvisioningTests and tenant isolation tests. Then verify against
the real pool and PostgreSQL:

1. Bootstrap the administrator and open the administration page.
2. Create tenants A and B; invite a different user into each and complete sign-in.
3. Inspect validated access-token tenant_id and exercise tenant-owned CRUD isolation.
4. Confirm an ordinary user cannot call tenant or user administration APIs.
5. Retry an invite: same tenant is recovered; another tenant gets 409 without changes.
6. Try an invalid address, disabled provisioning, unavailable Cognito, and explicit resend.
7. Suspend A: its API access is denied after the configured directory cache TTL.
8. Confirm public app clients cannot write custom:tenant_id.
9. Verify an existing pre-token trigger is preserved/integrated and invitations arrive.

Local mocks do not prove remote permissions, trigger setup, email delivery, or live
isolation. These checks require deployment and real users.
